Detailed Results of 2021 Population Census5
1.2 Age and Gender Structures 
In the past ten years, the birth rate of Macao has declined after an increase. Number of newborns rose 
durin
g 2012-2017, with the annual birth rates higher than 10 births per 1,000 population. Despite a drop in 
the birth rate after 2017, the youth population (aged 0-14) grew by 50.3% from ten years ago to 98,981, and 
its proportion in the total population expanded by 2.6 percentage points to 14.5%. 
The adult population (aged 15-64) was 500,277, an increase of 12.0% over 2011; yet, its proportion in 
the total population fell gradually from 80.8% in 2011 to 73.3% in 2021, as the growth of the adult population 
was slower than that of the youth and elderly populations. 
The elderly population (aged 65 and above) totalled 82,812, more than double (+107.2%) compared to 
2011; its proportion in the total population reached 12.1%, far higher than the 7.2% in 2011, indicating an 
acceleration of population ageing in Macao. 
Chart 2: Age Structure 
Total Population Local Population 
I
n view of a drop in the proportion of adult population and increases in the youth and elderly 
populations, the overall dependency ratio surged from 23.7% in 2011 to 36.3% in 2021. Moreover, the elderly 
dependency ratio jumped from 8.9% to 16.6%, representing an increase in dependency burden, from 11 
adults supporting 1 elderly person to 6 adults supporting 1 elderly person. In spite of a rise in the number of 
newborns compared to 2011, the total fertility rate of Macao remained lower than 1.4 for the past ten years, 
far below the replacement level of 2.1 proposed by the United Nations. With a low fertility rate and a rapid 
growth of the elderly population, the ageing ratio hiked from 60.7% in 2011 to 83.7% in 2021. 
When excluding non-resident workers and non-local students living in Macao, the various dependency 
ratios of the local population showed a marked rise, with the elderly dependency ratio standing at 21.2%, 
indicating that 5 adults supported 1 elderly person.  
