1.5  Marital Status 
The population aged 16 and above totalled 577,514; among them, 27.4% were never married and 4.5% 
were 
separated/divorced, up by 2.8 percentage points and 1.7 percentage points respectively as compared 
with the standardised rates in 2011. The proportions of those who were married (64.1%) and widowed (4.0%) 
dropped by 3.3 percentage points and 1.2 percentage points respectively from ten years ago. 
Chart 9: Marital Status of Population, 2011 and 2021 
As shown in Table 4, the singulate mean age at marriage (SMAM) rose gradually over the past two 
decades. In 2021, the SMAM of the total population was 30.0, an increase of 1.0 from ten years ago; the 
SMAM for men went up from 29.9 in 2011 to 30.8 in 2021, whereas that for women grew from 28.3 to 29.3, 
which indicated a trend towards late marriage for both genders.  
The SMAM of the local population rose by 0.8 over 2011 to 29.9; the SMAM for men was 30.9 while that 
for women was 29.1, up by 0.8 and 1.0 respectively. 
