Among the local population, the proportions of never-married males and married males stood at 26.2% 
and 69.5% respectively, both being higher than the corresponding shares of 23.8% and 63.3% for females. 
The proportion of men who were separated/divorced (2.9%) was smaller than the corresponding share for 
women (5.7%), as men were more likely to remarry than women. In view of longer lifespan of women, the 
proportion of widows was far higher than that of widowers. 
Chart 10: Marital Status of Local Population by Gender 
Chart 11 illustrated the proportions of the local population who were never married by age group, which 
indicated an increase in the proportions of never-married males and females in the majority of the age groups 
as compared to 2011. For the younger population aged 16-24, the proportions of never-married males and 
females both exceeded 90.0% as the majority of them (63.8%) were at the schooling stage. Owing to an 
increasing tendency towards marriage postponement, the proportions of never-married males and females 
aged 25-29 surged by 11.3 percentage points and 11.6 percentage points respectively from ten years ago, 
representing 74.9% and 63.3% of the respective populations of the same age group; meanwhile, the 
corresponding proportions for the population aged 30-34 were 37.4% and 26.6%, up by 4.3 percentage points 
and 1.8 percentage points respectively. 
The proportion of never-married persons in each age group diminished with age. With the population 
aged 40 and above, the proportion of never-married females was higher than that of males. The difference 
was most prominent in the age group of 55-59, with the proportion of never-married women (9.1%) 
exceeding that of never-married men (4.3%) by 4.8 percentage points; this was followed by the age group of 
40-44, with a difference of 3.9 percentage points. 
